  "body": "Closes #4857. OpenConjecture ID 2455.\n\nDean says that a graph with a minimum degree of `k` or more has a cycle. The length of that cycle divides by `k`. This is true for each integer `k` that is 3 or more.\n\n```lean\nanswer(sorry) ↔ ∀ (k : ℕ), 3 ≤ k → ∀ (V : Type) [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n  (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj], k ≤ G.minDegree →\n    ∃ m ∈ G.cycleLengths, k ∣ m\n```\n\nThe file gives five statements. One is the conjecture. One is the case `k = 5`, which is open. Three are the solved cases:\n\n| statement | case | source |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `dean_conjecture` | all `k ≥ 3` | Dean 1988 |\n| `variants.five` | `k = 5` | open |\n| `variants.three` | `k = 3` | Chen and Saito 1994 |\n| `variants.four` | `k = 4` | Dean, Lesniak and Saito 1993 |\n| `variants.six_le` | `k ≥ 6` | Liu, Ma and Zhao 2026 |\n\nTwo conditions prevent a trivial answer. A cycle has a length of 3 or more, so the divisor is never `0`. `SimpleGraph.minDegree` is `0` for a graph with no vertices and for a graph with no edges, so the hypothesis excludes both. The two `test` statements show this. Their axioms are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` only.\n\nThe file uses `SimpleGraph.cycleLengths` from `FormalConjecturesForMathlib`.\n\nBuilds clean. Five sorries, one for each research statement.",
